WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.047 --> 00:00:07.940
A client and I were deciding where a long list of agents would live, and we were landing on simple markdown agents in GitHub Copilot.

2
00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:12.332
But if they were ever built as code, LangGraph was the approved framework.

3
00:00:12.332 --> 00:00:16.367
So before that window closed, I found out what it would really take.

4
00:00:18.278 --> 00:00:22.065
Twenty-three agents in one evening only works with a plan.

5
00:00:22.065 --> 00:00:28.400
I built one shared core first: the graph kit, typed state, model calls, approvals and guardrails.

6
00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:31.142
Each agent is then a small package on top.

7
00:00:32.106 --> 00:00:39.372
Every agent follows the same graph shape: take in the request, ground it in sources, analyse, and check its own work.

8
00:00:39.372 --> 00:00:43.967
Only then, if it wants to change something, does it stop and ask a person.

9
00:00:45.344 --> 00:00:48.719
Here is a real one, exported straight from LangGraph.

10
00:00:48.719 --> 00:00:53.877
Green steps are plain Python, purple steps call the model, and amber is a person.

11
00:00:54.851 --> 00:00:56.390
And here is the wiring.

12
00:00:56.390 --> 00:00:59.001
Plain edges always go to the next step.

13
00:00:59.001 --> 00:01:01.678
Conditional edges let a function decide.

14
00:01:01.678 --> 00:01:05.091
After retrieving context, one hands off to fan_out.

15
00:01:06.063 --> 00:01:12.655
fan_out returns a list of Send objects: one extraction per document section, all running in parallel.

16
00:01:13.627 --> 00:01:21.712
A self-check then verifies every quote against its source, and a second conditional edge loops back, only for the sections that failed.

17
00:01:22.682 --> 00:01:26.450
Model calls return typed, validated objects, not free text.

18
00:01:26.450 --> 00:01:32.837
And if the content filter blocks a section, the agent records a finding, instead of failing the run.

19
00:01:33.799 --> 00:01:38.016
When an agent wants to change something, interrupt() pauses the graph.

20
00:01:38.016 --> 00:01:44.039
The checkpoint saves its state, and the run resumes exactly where it stopped, once a person decides.

21
00:01:45.015 --> 00:01:51.116
Because the model can be scripted out, close to 700 tests run in a couple of minutes, with no network.

22
00:01:51.683 --> 00:01:56.303
I had deployed agents to Foundry before, with Microsoft Agent Framework.

23
00:01:56.303 --> 00:01:59.896
This was my first time deploying LangGraph agents there.

24
00:02:01.734 --> 00:02:03.596
Deployment is generated.

25
00:02:03.596 --> 00:02:11.355
A build step makes one folder per agent, writes the azure.yaml, and one azd deploy publishes all 23.

26
00:02:11.653 --> 00:02:16.411
In Microsoft Foundry, under Agents, all 23 show up as hosted agents.

27
00:02:16.411 --> 00:02:22.149
Foundry built each container in the cloud, runs it, and versions every deployment.

28
00:02:22.502 --> 00:02:25.621
This is where Foundry makes them enterprise-ready.

29
00:02:25.621 --> 00:02:28.241
Open an agent, and select its Details tab.

30
00:02:32.236 --> 00:02:40.556
Each agent gets its own Entra agent identity, granted access to the project's models, so there are no keys or secrets in the code.

31
00:02:41.516 --> 00:02:47.511
Each one exposes a standard Responses endpoint, and only people with the right Azure role can call it.

32
00:02:48.482 --> 00:02:50.880
Guardrails sit on the model deployment.

33
00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:56.845
They block harmful content, detect jailbreaks, and check documents for indirect prompt injection.

34
00:03:00.644 --> 00:03:04.143
Back under Agents, every agent also has a Traces tab.

35
00:03:18.282 --> 00:03:19.793
Every request is traced.

36
00:03:19.793 --> 00:03:26.282
Open one, and you see each LangGraph step, and every model call underneath, with its timing and tokens.

37
00:03:28.056 --> 00:03:33.090
And Monitor rolls it all up across the agents: runs, tokens, and what it cost.

38
00:03:41.603 --> 00:03:49.515
Then I pointed Foundry's AI Red Teaming Agent at the deployed agents, with attacks hidden inside the documents they read.

39
00:03:49.515 --> 00:03:53.635
At the time of recording, its reports open in the classic view.

40
00:04:06.096 --> 00:04:07.790
It found one real failure.

41
00:04:07.790 --> 00:04:10.917
A planted harmful sentence became a requirement.

42
00:04:20.464 --> 00:04:28.037
The fix is layered: the content filter, a prompt rule, and a deterministic scan that drops anything built from harmful text.

43
00:04:28.312 --> 00:04:32.109
The re-scan found nothing, and that case is now part of the test suite.

44
00:04:43.035 --> 00:04:45.498
Finally, how does an app consume them?

45
00:04:45.498 --> 00:04:48.674
I extended Lumen, a chat app I had built earlier.

46
00:04:48.674 --> 00:04:54.768
It calls the agent's endpoint with the user's own Azure sign-in, and sends the attached files.

47
00:05:05.629 --> 00:05:09.680
When the agent pauses for a decision, Lumen shows an approval card.

48
00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:13.309
Approve it, and the agent picks up exactly where it stopped.

49
00:05:17.459 --> 00:05:25.815
On the code side, it is one provider file: build the request, call the Responses endpoint, and send the approval back.

50
00:05:25.815 --> 00:05:31.197
Foundry can also publish agents straight to Teams and Microsoft 365 Copilot.

51
00:05:31.778 --> 00:05:35.506
The client stayed with the simple approach, and that was fine.

52
00:05:35.506 --> 00:05:41.399
For me, it was a great way to put LangGraph and Microsoft Foundry through their paces, end to end.

53
00:05:42.216 --> 00:05:45.800
A long list of agent ideas, and one evening to find out.
